 See also [how to shard data among multiple remote storage systems](#sharding-among-remote-storages).
 
+## Automatic target count limiting
+
+Sometimes a scrape job backed by an auto-discovery source (a cloud API, an orchestrator such as Kubernetes, a service catalog, etc.)
+can briefly return an unexpectedly huge number of targets because of a misconfiguration on the discovery side
+(for example, a tag filter is accidentally removed or a catalog query becomes too broad). Since `vmagent` normally
+scrapes all the discovered targets at once, such a spike can significantly increase memory and CPU usage
+and degrade scraping for all the other jobs running on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+In order to protect against this, `vmagent` allows limiting the number of active targets a single `job_name` is allowed
+to have at once, no matter which service discovery mechanism (or combination of mechanisms) produced these targets:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag. This limit is applied individually to each `job_name`
+  across all the scrape configs.
+* Via `max_scrape_targets` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `max_scrape_targets` option allows overriding the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` on a per-`job_name` basis.
+  If `max_scrape_targets` is set to `0` or to a negative value, then it isn't applied to the given `job_name`,
+  even if the `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` command-line flag is set.
+
+By default, `-promscrape.maxScrapeTargetsPerJob` is set to `0`, e.g. the limit is disabled and the behavior is unaffected
+compared to versions of `vmagent`, which do not support this option.
+
+If a `job_name` has more targets than the configured limit after applying relabeling, then the excess targets
+are excluded from scraping instead of being scraped, crashing `vmagent`, or causing the whole `job_name` to be rejected.
+Targets, which are excluded because of this limit, are shown as `DROPPED (max_scrape_targets)` at the
+`/service-discovery` page, and the corresponding `job_name` is shown with the number
+of excluded targets at the `/targets` page. The `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets{scrape_job="<job_name>", status="excluded"}`
+metric exposes the current number of targets excluded from the given `job_name` because of this limit,
+while `vm_promscrape_scrape_pool_targets{scrape_job="<job_name>", status="up|down"}` metrics keep exposing
+the number of targets, which are actually being scraped.
+
